{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' to avoid induction, but this fails Popper's own terms because Popper explicitly proved that verisimilitude cannot be formally defined or calculated for false theories (which all scientific theories are, per falsificationism). Since the metric is logically incoherent and unusable for comparison, the preference for a 'better-corroborated' theory lacks the non-inductive rational basis the target claims, effectively smuggling in an unjustified inductive assumption that past survival predicts future closeness to truth.","premise":"Popper demonstrated that the formal definition of verisimilitude is incoherent when comparing two false theories, rendering it impossible to objectively determine which false theory is 'closer' to the truth.","successor_question":"If verisimilitude is formally undefinable for false theories, what specific non-inductive criterion does Popper offer to justify preferring one falsified theory over another without relying on the expectation of future success?","target":"c7cfd51c03fd34d6bef2793840f761426a4e9dc465061122ab338e21f401964f"}]}